<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="infrastructure-introduction">
  <title>Infrastructure</title>
  <section xml:id="infrastructure-introduction-introduction">
    <title>Introduction</title>
    <p>Programming a modern game can have a wide range of required resources.
        You could make a simple game such as text based computer game that only 
        requires a 
        programming language that allows you to store, retrieve, display, 
        and prompt for text or you could make a 3D massively multiplayer
        online role playing game (<term>MMORPG</term>) that requires 3D graphics programs,
        a game engine, network infrastructure, cloud based storage, and more
        (as well as the staff that can manage and use all of the tools).
        Or you could make a game somewhere in between those two extremes.
    </p>
    <p>This part of the book will discuss an infrastructure that can support
        moderately complicated single console games intended to be distributed 
        to a wide audience over the web. The infrastructure can also support
        networked multiplayer games over a local area network (LAN).
    </p>
    <p>
        When you decide to develop your games on your own you'll need to make
        infrastructure decisions about the <term>game engine</term>, 
        <term>artwork tools</term> and/or <term>artwork sources</term>, 
        <term>version control software</term>, <term>web hosting</term>,
        and <term>distribution</term>. Since you are using this book, either you
        or your instructor has decided to use the Godot game engine with
        it's domain-specific language (DSL) GDScript. Strictly speaking
        the <term>game engine</term> is the software that puts together
        all of the different parts of the game and runs the game on one or
        more <term>platforms</term>. However, in order to support the game
        engine, there is typically an <term>integrated development environment</term>
        (<term>IDE</term>) that allows you to build the game. For a game engine this
        includes a place to edit source code, add graphics and other assets,
        add animation, edit physics properties, etc. Godot uses the
        git <term>version control software</term> to keep track of revisions.
    </p>
    <p>
        In addition to Godot, this book will describe GitHub as both a 
        <term>web hosting</term> and a <term>distribution</term> solution.
        However, there are many other options, and GitHub was chosen because
        of it's integration with GitHub Education that provides free services
        to active Faculty and Students with verification.
    </p>
    
</section>
  </chapter>
